        ":question/id": "4194dc2e-82ad-5109-91f4-d383eed4b18e",
        ":question/problem": "Calculate $\\int {x}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We apply the power rule with $n=6$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{6+1}}{6+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{7}}{7}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{7}}{7}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{7}}{7})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}\\cdot 7{x}^{6} & = \\\\ {x}^{6}\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
      "role": "independently authored inventory",
      "relationship_to_source_templates": "Previously generated unit-coefficient power-rule instance; not original Math Academy evidence."
    },
