        ":question/id": "4fc55d29-07c8-58f3-8bc8-d1223d0589d0",
        ":question/problem": "Calculate $\\int {x}^{7}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Include the arbitrary constant of integration.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We apply the power rule with $n=7$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{7}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{7+1}}{7+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{8}}{8}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{8}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{8})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{8})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 8{x}^{7} & = \\\\ {x}^{7}\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
