        ":question/worked-solution": "We apply the power rule with $n=5$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{5+1}}{5+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{6})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 6{x}^{5} & = \\\\ {x}^{5}\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$"
